# Line endings are not content. The repository stores LF and every checkout
# gets LF, on Windows as much as anywhere else.
#
# Without this a Windows checkout carries CRLF, and that stays invisible until
# something reads a file as text rather than as lines. It cost one green-here,
# red-on-CI afternoon already: a test that reads its own source and rejoins the
# lines the formatter wrapped matched on the newline, so the join missed and a
# wrapped constant parsed as empty. Readers that tolerate either ending are
# still worth writing; this is what keeps the question from coming up.
#
# It is also what keeps the freeze checkable. Every claims ledger stamps the
# SHA-256 of its document's bytes, and line-ending conversion on checkout would
# rewrite those bytes and report all nine as a mismatch against files nobody
# edited. Leave this file alone.
#
# Nothing here wants CRLF. The one PowerShell script runs the same either way,
# and the hook the user copies into Saved Games is read by the sim's own Lua,
# which does not care.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Shell scripts need LF whatever an editor does to them.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Never munge a binary someone adds by hand. Extracts and packed files are
# ignored rather than committed, but a forced add must not corrupt one.
*.sqlite binary
*.bin    binary
*.exe    binary
